+# Local MAPF Online Integration Design
+
+## Scope
+
+This experimental, opt-in integration evaluates whether the bounded
+`rmf_traffic::agv::LocalConflictResolver` at commit `e569de3` can coordinate
+two to four active `GoToPlace` events in one Fleet Adapter process. It does not
+change the VDA5050 adapter, add an external arbiter, deploy to real robots, or
+claim that fallback to legacy RMF resolves legacy deadlocks.
+
+The hard solver limit is four agents. Runtime configuration may select a lower
+limit but cannot raise it. More than four eligible local intents, unsupported
+inputs, external-fleet conflicts, repeated stale results, or exhausted budgets
+return control to the existing RMF planning and negotiation paths while
+preserving each physical robot's stationary schedule occupancy.
+
+## Ownership and active intents
+
+`FleetUpdateHandle::Implementation` owns one `LocalJointPlanningCoordinator`
+for its process and fleet. Only the currently active `GoToPlace` event may
+register an intent. An intent contains participant ID, current `StartSet`, the
+chosen original task goal, planner, request generation, location observation,
+and weak callbacks for result application and fallback. Pending future tasks
+are never registered.
+
+Each participant has one navigation owner: ordinary planning, local quiescing,
+local solving, local applying, or negotiation. Ownership transitions run on
+the fleet worker. A negotiation response invalidates a local generation,
+cancels its solver, and waits for local cleanup before the legacy responder is
+allowed to start a command. Repeated negotiation preemption is metered and
+eventually suppresses local retries for that active generation.
+
+## Quiescence and observations
+
+Calling `RobotCommandHandle::stop()` requests quiescence but does not prove it.
+The coordinator asynchronously waits for all of the following:
+
+- a state report newer than the stop request and within a configurable
+  freshness window;
+- non-driving/velocity-below-threshold evidence when the command integration
+  supplies it;
+- pose displacement and yaw below configurable tolerances for a configurable
+  settling period;
+- a valid, non-empty `StartSet` consistent with the stable pose.
+
+The fleet worker is never blocked while waiting. Timeout, stale telemetry,
+disconnect, or invalid starts aborts local application and invokes legacy
+fallback. Identical repeated reports refresh telemetry but do not count as
+movement. Material translation, rotation, lane/waypoint change, goal change,
+cancel, or generation change invalidates a solve.
+
+## Stationary schedule occupancy
+
+After physical quiescence, each participant receives a stationary itinerary at
+its reported pose. It begins at the observation time and extends beyond the
+local solve/apply deadline. This replaces the obsolete moving itinerary, so a
+stopped robot neither appears to keep moving nor disappears. Cleanup and
+fallback replace the hold with a new plan or extend it; they never clear the
+physical occupancy without a replacement.
+
+External participants remain in the schedule snapshot and are treated as
+obstacles by `LocalConflictResolver`. They are never included in the local
+commit group.
+
+## Solving and stale validation
+
+The coordinator captures all starts, goals, planner pointers, participant IDs,
+generations, semantic location signatures, and one schedule snapshot on the
+fleet worker. `LocalConflictResolver::solve()` runs on a separate worker. Its
+result returns to the fleet worker.
+
+Before applying, every participant is revalidated for generation, active goal,
+cancel state, ownership, report freshness, stable pose tolerance, and valid
+starts. Plan start times that are already in the past are not shifted blindly:
+the group is re-snapshotted and re-solved within a bounded retry count. The
+final projected itineraries are checked for pairwise conflicts and conflicts
+with the current external schedule snapshot immediately before registration.
+
+## Schedule-first, non-atomic application
+
+Application has two explicitly non-atomic stages:
+
+1. Allocate plan IDs, project each full itinerary, and register every schedule
+   itinerary while all robots remain stopped.
+2. Create and start each `ExecutePlan` without performing duplicate schedule
+   registration.
+
+Both stages expose test-only failure injection. If schedule registration fails,
+all participants remain stopped, stationary occupancy is restored, and legacy
+replanning is requested. If execution creation/start fails after some robots
+have begun, all affected robots are stopped again, physical quiescence is
+confirmed asynchronously, stationary occupancy is restored, and legacy
+replanning resumes. The guarantee is therefore “no command starts before every
+schedule registration succeeds”, not transactional or atomic application.
+
+`ExecutePlan` preparation must preserve existing dependency, completion,
+cancel, and task-event semantics exactly once. Local plans terminate at the
+original task goal. Holding/bay waypoints are intermediate motion and never
+complete the task.
+
+## Integration boundaries
+
+`GoToPlace::_find_plan()` registers or refreshes the current active intent
+before ordinary initial planning and replanning. If a local group is accepted,
+individual planning is suspended for that generation. Otherwise the existing
+`FindPath` path proceeds unchanged.
+
+`GoToPlace::_respond()` requests negotiation ownership. It invalidates and
+cleans up any local cycle before calling the existing `services::Negotiate`
+path. The PoC does not attempt a multi-participant submission across separate
+negotiation tables.
+
+Cancel, kill, interrupt, goal reselection, and completed task unregister their
+intent and invalidate callbacks. The feature is disabled by default.
+
+## Verification
+
+Unit tests cover semantic pose revisions, freshness and stop settling,
+stationary occupancy, stale result rejection, past-start retry, collision
+revalidation, both failure injection stages, negotiation ownership, repeated
+preemption, greater-than-four fallback, external participants, and original
+goal completion.
+
+Docker/MQTT experiments separately record simulator poses, minimum separation,
+task completion, maximum wait, negotiation/replan/local-solve counts, stale
+discard reasons, plan IDs, VDA5050 order IDs, and unsupported cases. Required
+experiments include A/B with dynamic C and D, replans at multiple motion
+phases, report delay, speed variation, pause, cancellation, goal change, idle
+occupancy, terminal occupancy, and injected partial application failures.
+
